== The Increase Of Faith

#smallcaps[How can we gain an increase of faith]? This is a very earnest question to many. They say they want to believe, but cannot. A great deal of nonsense is talked on this subject. Let us be strictly practical in dealing with it. Common sense is as much needed in religion as anywhere else. “What am I to do in order to believe?” Someone who was asked the best way to do a certain simple thing replied that the best way to do it was to do it at once. We waste time discussing methods when the action is simple. The shortest way to believe is to believe. If the Holy Spirit has made you open-minded, you will believe as soon as the truth is set before you. You will believe it because it is true. The gospel command is clear: “Believe in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved.” It is idle to dodge this with questions and quibbles. The order is plain; let it be obeyed.

But still, #emph[if you have a difficulty, take it to God in prayer]. Tell the great Father exactly what it is that puzzles you, and beg him by his Holy Spirit to solve the question. If I cannot believe a statement in a book, I am glad to ask the author what he means by it; and if he is an honest man, his explanation will satisfy me: how much more will the divine explanation of the hard points of Scripture satisfy the heart of the true seeker. The Lord is willing to make himself known; go to him and see if it is not so. Go at once to your inner room, and cry, “O Holy Spirit, lead me into the truth. What I do not know, teach me.”

Furthermore, if faith seems difficult, it may be that God the Holy Spirit will enable you to believe if you #emph[hear very often and earnestly what you are commanded to believe]. We believe many things because we have heard them so often. Do you not find it so in everyday life, that if you hear a thing fifty times a day, at last you come to believe it? Some men have come to believe very unlikely statements this way: and so I do not wonder that the good Spirit often blesses the method of hearing the truth often, and uses it to work faith in what is to be believed. It is written, “Faith comes by hearing,” so hear often. If I hear the gospel earnestly and attentively, one of these days I will find myself believing what I hear, through the blessed working of the Spirit of God on my mind. Only make sure that you hear #emph[the gospel], and do not distract your mind by hearing or reading what is meant to shake you.

If that, however, should seem poor advice, I would add next, #emph[consider the testimony of others]. The Samaritans believed because of what the woman told them about Jesus. Many of our beliefs arise from the testimony of others. I believe that there is such a country as Japan: I have never seen it, and yet I believe that there is such a place because others have been there. I believe that I will die: I have never died, but a great many whom I once knew have done so, and therefore I am sure that I will die too; the testimony of many convinces me of that fact. Listen, then, to those who tell you how they were saved, how they were pardoned, how they were changed in character. If you look into the matter, you will find that somebody just like yourself has been saved. If you have been a thief, you will find that a thief rejoiced to wash away his sin in the fountain of Christ’s blood. If, sadly, you have been unchaste, you will find that men and women who have fallen in that way have been cleansed and changed. If you are in despair, you have only to get among God’s people and ask around a little, and you will find that some of the saints have been in as much despair as you are, and they will be pleased to tell you how the Lord delivered them. As you listen to one after another of those who have tested the word of God and found it true, the divine Spirit will lead you to believe. Have you not heard of the African who was told by the missionary that water sometimes became so hard that a man could walk on it? He declared that he believed a great many things the missionary had told him; but he would never believe that. When he came to England, it happened that one frosty day he saw the river frozen, but he would not venture onto it. He knew that it was a deep river, and he felt certain that he would drown if he ventured onto it. He could not be brought to walk on the frozen water till his friend and many others went onto it: then he was persuaded, and trusted himself where others had safely ventured. So, perhaps, as you see others believe in the Lamb of God, and notice their joy and peace, you will yourself be gently led to believe. The experience of others is one of God’s ways of helping us to faith. However that may be, you must either believe in Jesus or die; there is no hope for you but in him.

A better plan still is this — #emph[note the authority on which you are commanded to believe], and this will greatly help you to faith. The authority is not mine, or you might well reject it. It is not even the Pope’s, or you might question it. But you are commanded to believe on the authority of God himself. #emph[He] tells you to believe in Jesus Christ, and you must not refuse to obey your Maker. The foreman of a certain factory in the North had often heard the Gospel, but he was troubled by the fear that he had no right to come to Christ. His good master one day sent a card around the factory — “Come to my house immediately after work.” The foreman appeared at his master’s door, and the master came out and said rather roughly, “What do you want, John, troubling me at this hour? Work is done; what right have you to be here?” “Sir,” he said, “I had a card from you saying that I was to come after work.” “Do you mean to say that just because you had a card from me you may come up to my house and call me out after business hours?” “Well, sir,” replied the foreman, “I do not understand you, but it seems to me that, as you sent for me, I had a right to come.” “Come in, John,” said his master, “I have another message I want to read to you”; and he sat down and read these words: “Come to Me, all you#super[+] who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you#super[+] rest.” “Do you think, after such a message from Christ, that you can be wrong in coming to him?” The poor man saw it all at once, and believed in the Lord Jesus to eternal life, because he saw that he had good warrant and authority for believing. So do you, poor soul! You have good authority for coming to Christ, for the Lord himself tells you to trust him.

If that does not breed faith in you, #emph[think over what it is that you have to believe] — that the Lord Jesus Christ suffered in the place and stead of sinners, and is able to save all who trust him. Why, this is the most blessed fact that men were ever told to believe; the most suitable, the most comforting, the most divine truth that was ever set before mortal minds. I advise you to think much about it, and search out the grace and love it contains. Study the four Gospels, study Paul’s letters, and then see if the message is not so credible that you are forced to believe it.

If that does not work, then #emph[think about the person of Jesus Christ] — think of #emph[who] he is, and #emph[what] he did, and #emph[where] he is, and #emph[what] he is. How can you doubt #emph[him]? It is cruel to distrust the ever truthful Jesus. He has done nothing to deserve distrust; on the contrary, it should be easy to rely on him. Why crucify him again by unbelief? Is this not crowning him with thorns again, and spitting on him again? What! Is he not to be trusted? What worse insult did the soldiers heap on him than this? They made him a martyr; but you make him a liar — this is far worse. Do not ask, #emph[How can I believe]? But answer another question — #emph[How can you disbelieve]?

If none of these things helps, then there is something wrong with you altogether, and my last word is, #emph[submit yourself to God]! Prejudice or pride lies at the bottom of this unbelief. May the Spirit of God take away your enmity and make you yield. You are a rebel, a proud rebel, and that is why you do not believe your God. Give up your rebellion; throw down your weapons; yield without conditions; surrender to your King. I believe that no soul ever threw up its hands in self-despair and cried, “Lord, I yield,” without finding that faith soon became easy to it. It is because you still have a quarrel with God, and are determined to have your own will and your own way, that you cannot believe. “How can you#super[+] believe,” said Christ, “if you#super[+] accept glory from one another?” Proud self creates unbelief. Submit, O man. Yield to your God, and then you will sweetly believe in your Savior. May the Holy Spirit now work secretly but effectually with you, and bring you at this very moment to believe in the Lord Jesus! Amen.
